.UPDATE...
Issued at 930 AM PDT Tue Jul 28 2026

Water vapor imagery shows a relatively deep trough of low
pressure situated along the West Coast this morning, with a couple
of embedded short waves within this trough. The multiple water
vapor channels also show very dry air situated over California
this morning at the various levels. As a result, there is no
cloud cover over the state, with the exception of the marine layer
along the immediate coast. The 12z KOAK sounding shows that the
marine layer is approximately 1700 feet deep, with an impressive
inversion of nearly 12 deg above the marine layer. This warmer
layer could be seen in some of the thermal belted areas, where
overnight low temperatures stayed in the mid 60s to lower 70s,
such as Highlands Peak. Meanwhile, the drier air over the region
allow for some decent radiative cooling last night away from the
coast, with most locations staring off in the upper 40s to 50s.
The marine layer helped moderate some of the coastal locations, so
temperatures were more in the mid 50s to lower 60s.

At this time, temperatures are running similar to this time
yesterday, and expect this trend to continue through the day.
Gusty onshore winds will also develop through the day, and this
will help continue to moderate temperatures along the coast,
while inland areas will still be seasonally warm. Current forecast
looks on track, with no updates are planned for the forecast at
this time. Palmer

