.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1221 AM PDT Tue Jul 28 2026
(Today and tonight)

If you read the evening AFD update micro-climates were
highlighted. Seems fitting to kick off this AFD with another
micro-climate highlight. If one looks at the overnight satellite
imagery they`ll notice only patchy stratus developing. I do
expect more by morning, but patchy for now. So why is it patchy,
pockets of offshore flow most notably over the N Bay and along the
Big Sur coast. Offshore flow is a drying and warming wind for our
region. Taking a closer look at Big Sur there are two sensors
called Highlands Peak. It`s close to midnight and both are
reporting temperatures near 80 degrees. N to NE flow leads to
compressional warming as the air descends the Santa Lucias, hence
the warm temperatures.

Tuesday will be another pleasant day weather wise most areas. The
longwave pattern continues to advertise broad upper level
troughing just offshore and upper level ridging to the SE.
Similar to Monday we`ll see a large spread of temps from the
coast to the interior. Onshore flow will keep coast/bays in check
with highs in the 60s/70s. Farther inland with marine layer
influence will see much warmer temperatures with highs in the
upper 80s to near 100.

Given the temperature contrast expect another round of breezy
afternoon winds in the valleys and inland gaps/passes.

Tuesday night the upper level trough begins to ease with upper
level ridging building in from the east. As such, a more stable
marine layer will develop with slightly better stratus coverage.
Above the marine layer temperatures will be on the milder side.

