.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1221 AM PDT Tue Jul 28 2026
(Wednesday through Monday)

Wednesday will be a transition day as the longwave pattern shifts
from nearby trough to a building ridge. It should be noted the
building ridge from the east brought very dangerous heat to the
Desert SW. Not expecting that here, but a marked warm up
nonetheless. Expect an initial jump in temps of 3-5 degrees from
Tuesday to Wednesday. Still seeing some night/morning marine
stratus given lingering onshore flow. Additionally, we will
continue to see the large temp spread from the coast to the
interior.

Thursday through Monday - The heat dial gets turned up each day
and peaks over the weekend before moderating early next week. The
very warm upper level ridge to the east will overspread the region
for much of the long term. Projected 850mb temps will be in the
mid to upper 20s, which when fully mixed will easily support temps
in the 105-110 range. However, onshore flow remains at the
surface. Therefore, the fullest potential of the airmass will only
be realized across the interior. That`s the key to keeping the
coastal areas and bays in check temp-wise - onshore flow. Some
guidance does show periods of easing onshore flow and that has
bust potential written all over it with coastal areas ending up
warmer than forecast. Confidence isn`t high enough to fully buy
that solution just yet. Much higher confidence exists for
interior locations to experience triple digit heat. Given the
very mild airmass thermal belts will be very active too with
minor relief in the hills overnight. Could easily see temps
staying in the upper 60s to mid 70s in the hills. Therefore, as
daytime temperatures increase and less nighttime relief is
observed overall HeatRisk increases. While most areas without any
marine influence will be moderate HeatRisk from Thursday through
early next week there will be pockets of Major to Extreme for
interior Monterey/San Benito. As the weekend forecast becomes more
clear will need to take a look to see if a heat advisory is
needed.

Longer range guidance diverges on extent of the heat, but some
guidance keep the heat going for much of next week.

