.BEACHES...
Issued at 145 PM PDT Sun Jul 26 2026

Distant tropical cyclones in the Eastern Pacific will continue to
generate 16-18 second (long-period) southwesterly swell which has
reached the coastline of the San Francisco Bay Area and Central
Coast. As such, sneaker waves and strong rip currents are the
primary concerns. Breaking waves can reach up to 12 feet along
some south and southwest facing beaches and break points, such as
Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of Stinson or Ocean Beach. This
can be particularly surprising when wave heights increase suddenly
after a period of seemingly tranquil wave action. In fact,
sneaker waves can have 20+ minutes in between the larger sets
(breaking waves) with significant "flat/calm looking" times
between them. Due to the long period, sneaker waves are powerful
and can catch beachgoers by surprise, resulting in people and pets
being swept into the cold, turbulent ocean. Rip currents are
strong enough to pull even the strongest swimmers away from shore.
Check marine conditions before you head out! Swim near a
lifeguard, if possible. Maintain a safe distance from the water,
and stay away from jetties, piers, beachside rocks, and other
waterside infrastructure. Never turn your back to the ocean and if
you have small children or pets at the beach, please keep an eye
on them. Hazardous beach conditions are anticipated to persist
through Saturday afternoon.

